window.PAST = {"listing":"https://scag.ca.gov/rhna-and-housing-element-reform-subcommittee-past-agendas","pdfBase":"https://scag.ca.gov/sites/default/files/2024-05/","downloaded":"2026-11-06","meetings":[{"id":"m20190603","iso":"2019-06-03","date":"Jun 3, 2019","label":"6th Cycle RHNA Subcommittee, 10:00 AM","stem":"rhnasub060319fullagn","pdfPages":81,"place":"SCAG Main Office, 900 Wilshire Blvd., Ste. 1700, Los Angeles (videoconference sites incl. the Orange County Regional Office)","chair":"Hon. Peggy Huang (Yorba Linda, TCA; Orange County alternate)","overview":["The Subcommittee was asked to recommend that the CEHD Committee approve SCAG's RHNA consultation package to HCD (action item 5).","Staff laid out a recommended allocation methodology (information item 6): existing need split 70% by population and 30% by population in high quality transit areas, with a 110% social equity adjustment; projected need from household growth plus vacancy and replacement need, with a 150% adjustment.","Two illustrative cities (City A and City B, each 65,000 people) show how it would work: City A totals 1,420 units and City B 1,965. The packet says these use assumed regional totals (250,000 existing, 400,000 projected) for illustration only.","The minutes of the May 6, 2019 meeting are included. They record comments from the City of Irvine's senior planner, a Mission Viejo representative and an Orange County resident."],"votes":{"text":"The votes at this meeting are in the next meeting's minutes, which are not in the set of packets downloaded. The packet does record the May 6, 2019 votes (see Item 1).","src":null},"items":[{"id":"pg_n","mtg":"m20190603","grp":"Meeting notice and members","title":"Meeting notice and videoconference sites","pages":[1,2],"sum":"Regular meeting, Monday June 3, 2019, 10:00 AM to 12:00 PM, SCAG Main Office (900 Wilshire Blvd., Ste. 1700, Los Angeles) with video and teleconference sites, including the SCAG Orange County Regional Office (600 S. Main St., Orange) and Long Beach and Simi Valley city halls. Webcast was view-only.","detail":["Questions and comments on RHNA go to housing@scag.ca.gov","The SCAG Ventura County Regional Office was closed until further notice"],"qs":[],"rel":["pg_m"],"outcome":""},{"id":"pg_m","mtg":"m20190603","grp":"Meeting notice and members","title":"Subcommittee membership (RHNA 6th cycle)","pages":[3,3],"sum":"Six voting county seats plus three non-voting ex-officio members. Orange County: Hon. Wendy Bucknum (Mission Viejo) as primary and Chair Peggy Huang (Yorba Linda, TCA) as alternate. Ex-officio: Paavo Monkkonen (UCLA, academia), Cesar Covarrubias (Kennedy Commission, non-profit) and Jeff Montejano (BIA of Southern California, building industry).","detail":[],"qs":[],"rel":["pg_n"],"outcome":""},{"id":"pg_a","mtg":"m20190603","grp":"Agenda","title":"Agenda","pages":[5,6],"sum":"Consent calendar: Item 1 minutes of May 6, 2019; Item 2 6th Cycle RHNA Timeline; Item 3 RHNA Subcommittee Topic Outlook; Item 4 Summary of Written Comments Received. Action item 5: RHNA Consultation Package to HCD (Kevin Kane, 20 minutes). Information item 6: Proposed RHNA Methodology Distribution: Existing and Projected Needs and Social Equity (Ma'Ayn Johnson, 60 minutes). The Chair invited members to the June 6 CEHD meeting, where the Subcommittee's consultation package recommendation would be acted on.","detail":["Next regular meeting: July 1, 2019, 10 AM"],"qs":[],"rel":["pg_5","pg_6"],"outcome":""},{"id":"pg_1","mtg":"m20190603","grp":"Consent calendar","title":"Item 1. Minutes of the May 6, 2019 meeting","pages":[7,12],"sum":"Record of the previous meeting. The Subcommittee recommended SCAG's approach for the HCD consultation process to CEHD, 6 ayes and no noes or abstentions. It received the panel-of-experts recap (Kevin Kane), heard public comment, and held a discussion of how to distribute existing need. Several members spoke for phasing the existing-need portion over more than one cycle (Finlay, Huang); Paavo Monkkonen warned it could shift attention away from existing need.","detail":["Marika Poynter, City of Irvine senior planner, asked SCAG to propose a regional determination of 430,000 for the 6th cycle, to take the existing-need calculations out of the HCD consultation package, and to hold off acting on the social equity adjustment until HCD gives a regional total. She said Irvine proposes a 110% social equity adjustment, as in the 4th and 5th cycles.","Deborah Diep (Center for Demographic Research, Cal State Fullerton) commented on how much the panel of experts differed on the size of existing need","Gail Shiomoto-Lohr (Mission Viejo) asked staff to look at each jurisdiction's own relationship to indicators such as overcrowding and cost burden, and described the difficulty cities face meeting need under state site and zoning rules","Chair Huang adjourned at 12:11 PM; Margaret Finlay asked whether the topics warranted a longer meeting"],"qs":["The Irvine planner asked for existing-need numbers to be separated from the HCD package. Where did that argument end up in the final determination?","Phasing existing need across cycles was discussed in 2019. Does the 7th cycle determination or methodology use anything like it?"],"rel":["pg_5","pg_6","pf_6"],"outcome":"6 ayes, no noes, no abstentions on the motion to recommend SCAG's approach to CEHD (Predmore, Finlay, Huang, Bailey, Jahn, Judge)."},{"id":"pg_2","mtg":"m20190603","grp":"Consent calendar","title":"Item 2. 6th Cycle RHNA Timeline (receive and file)","pages":[13,14],"sum":"One-page timeline. The 6th cycle covers the housing element planning period of October 2021 through October 2029, and 6th cycle housing elements are due to HCD in October 2021. Milestones include the regional determination, methodology development, the draft allocation, appeals and the final allocation.","detail":["Meetings of the RHNA Subcommittee are held on the first Monday of each month unless otherwise noted"],"qs":[],"rel":["pf_2"],"outcome":""},{"id":"pg_3","mtg":"m20190603","grp":"Consent calendar","title":"Item 3. RHNA Subcommittee Topic Outlook (receive and file)","pages":[15,16],"sum":"The schedule of upcoming subcommittee topics (revised 05/22/19), receive and file.","detail":[],"qs":[],"rel":["pg_2"],"outcome":""},{"id":"pg_4","mtg":"m20190603","grp":"Consent calendar","title":"Item 4. Summary of Written Comments Received (as of 05/06/19)","pages":[17,18],"sum":"A table of comments sent to SCAG, with date, name, organization, topic and a one-line summary. Entries include the City of Mission Viejo (Gail Shiomoto-Lohr, Dec 2, 2018) on the charter, subregional delegation and the growth forecast, and the City of Beverly Hills.","detail":[],"qs":[],"rel":["pf_4"],"outcome":""},{"id":"pg_5","mtg":"m20190603","grp":"Action item","title":"Item 5. RHNA Consultation Package to HCD (staff report and technical appendix)","pages":[19,36],"sum":"Recommended action: recommend that CEHD approve SCAG's consultation package to HCD. SCAG proposes a clear line between housing need from projected population growth (from the RTP/SCS growth forecast) and \"existing need\" (the backlog), because 2017 and 2018 laws added data elements closely related to existing need. The draft package was approved by the Subcommittee on May 6; staff met HCD on May 9 and HCD said the new laws were meant to address the production backlog explicitly. HCD's determination was expected in August 2019.","detail":["Table 1 (p.24): projected households grow by 594,737 from Jan 2018 to Oct 2029 (11.75 years); adjustments for vacancy (6,113), replacement (2,438) and overcrowding (13,857), less 4,310 for growth on tribal lands, total 612,836, which works out to 430,289 units over the 8.25-year RHNA projection period (Jul 1, 2021 to Oct 1, 2029)","Eight matters for HCD to consider, including: use of cost burden as an input, historical comparison, correlation and double-counting between measures of existing need, phasing existing need beyond a single cycle, and sites and zoning issues","The Subcommittee stressed double-counting and phasing on May 6 and asked staff to clarify with HCD matters such as student housing","SCAG notes RHNA is a planning target and does not require jurisdictions or COGs to build housing (p.34)"],"qs":["The package argued cost burden and overcrowding overlap and risk double-counting. How did HCD treat that in the 6th cycle determination, and in the 7th (where the cost-burden adjustment is now contested)?","SCAG's 2019 estimate of growth-driven need was 430,289 units over 8.25 years. What did HCD finally determine for the 6th cycle?"],"rel":["pg_6","pg_1","d_meth","tab_cost"],"outcome":"The vote on this item is not in the downloaded packets."},{"id":"pg_6","mtg":"m20190603","grp":"Information item","title":"Item 6. Proposed RHNA Methodology Distribution: Existing and Projected Needs and Social Equity (staff report)","pages":[37,58],"sum":"Staff recommend a three-step methodology. Step 1, existing need: 70% distributed by a jurisdiction's share of regional population and 30% by its share of the population living in high quality transit areas (HQTAs), then a 110% social equity adjustment into three income categories. Step 2, projected need: share of regional household growth (from local input), plus future vacancy need (1.5% owner, 5.0% renter) and a replacement need from a city survey, then a 150% social equity adjustment into four categories. Step 3: add the two. For information; staff sought direction and planned a proposed methodology for July 1, then CEHD and the Regional Council on Aug 5, 2019.","detail":["Illustration with a regional existing need of 250,000 and projected need of 400,000: City A (38% of its acreage in an HQTA) gets 1,420 units (484 very low, 312 low, 360 moderate, 264 above moderate); City B (no HQTAs) gets 1,965 (643, 413, 439, 471)","City A's existing need is 606 from population share plus 274 from HQTA share; City B's is 606 plus 0","Staff say this is a break from past cycles, which focused mainly on projected need (p.44)","Staff report the 110% adjustment was used in the 4th and 5th cycles and recommend it for existing need only; a larger 150% adjustment is recommended for projected need"],"qs":["The methodology is a function of two choices (70/30 and 110%/150%). Which of those did the 6th cycle keep, and what does the 7th cycle schedule (proposed methodology Mar 11, 2027) put back on the table?","City B, with no HQTAs, still receives a higher total (1,965 vs 1,420) in the illustration. Which Orange County cities resemble City A and which resemble City B?"],"rel":["pg_5","pf_6","pe_4","d_meth"],"outcome":"This was an information item; the packet records no vote."},{"id":"pg_att","mtg":"m20190603","grp":"Attachments to Item 6","title":"Attachment: jurisdiction data tables and county maps","pages":[59,67],"sum":"Pages 59 to 62 are tables by county, subregion and city (headings show 2018 population, 2017 households, permits issued per 1,000 population and overcrowded households). Pages 63 to 67 are county maps naming cities. The text in this part of the PDF is encoded or image-only, so the figures could not be read and are not summarised here.","detail":[],"qs":[],"rel":["pg_6"],"outcome":""},{"id":"pg_ppt","mtg":"m20190603","grp":"Attachments to Item 6","title":"PowerPoint: Proposed RHNA Distribution Methodology (13 slides)","pages":[69,81],"sum":"Slide headings: Proposed RHNA Distribution Methodology and RHNA Milestones; Objectives of RHNA; Step 1 Determining Existing Need (30% / 70%); Step 2 Determining Projected Housing Need; Step 3 Total RHNA Allocation; City A and City B walk-throughs of Steps 1 and 2 (including the social equity adjustment chart and future vacancy need); The Case for Existing Housing Need Methodology; Next Steps.","detail":["Slide bullets are images in the PDF, so only headings and some labels could be read"],"qs":[],"rel":["pg_6"],"outcome":""}]},{"id":"m20190401","iso":"2019-04-01","date":"Apr 1, 2019","label":"6th Cycle RHNA Subcommittee, 10:00 AM","stem":"rhnasub040119fullagn","pdfPages":48,"place":"SCAG Main Office, 900 Wilshire Blvd., Ste. 1700, Los Angeles","chair":"Hon. Peggy Huang (Yorba Linda, TCA; Orange County alternate)","overview":["HCD's Megan Kirkeby briefed the Subcommittee on the RHNA process and statewide legislative and budget updates (item 5).","Staff sought input on how to distribute existing housing need and apply a social equity adjustment (item 6). They looked at transit access, jobs-housing fit and opportunity indices, and at how other councils of governments did it.","The Subcommittee had said on March 4 that the 110% adjustment from the 4th and 5th cycles was a good starting point but not enough to address communities with a high concentration of low income households.","Orange County's median household income was $81,851 (ACS 2013 to 2017), second highest of the six counties after Ventura ($81,972)."],"votes":{"text":"The votes at this meeting would be in the May 6, 2019 minutes, which are in the next packet but not in the downloaded set. The packet does record the March 4, 2019 votes (see Item 1).","src":null},"items":[{"id":"pf_n","mtg":"m20190401","grp":"Meeting notice and members","title":"Meeting notice, date and time note, videoconference sites","pages":[1,3],"sum":"Monday April 1, 2019, 10:00 AM to 12:00 PM at SCAG's Los Angeles office, with video sites including the Orange County Regional Office and teleconference sites in Big Bear Lake, Long Beach and Simi Valley. The cover carries a \"Please Note Date and Time\" notice.","detail":[],"qs":[],"rel":["pf_m"],"outcome":""},{"id":"pf_m","mtg":"m20190401","grp":"Meeting notice and members","title":"Subcommittee membership (RHNA 6th cycle)","pages":[5,5],"sum":"Orange County: Hon. Wendy Bucknum (Mission Viejo) as primary and Chair Peggy Huang (Yorba Linda, TCA) as alternate. Ex-officio: Paavo Monkkonen (UCLA), Cesar Covarrubias (Kennedy Commission), Jeff Montejano (BIA of Southern California).","detail":[],"qs":[],"rel":["pf_n"],"outcome":""},{"id":"pf_a","mtg":"m20190401","grp":"Agenda","title":"Agenda","pages":[7,8],"sum":"Includes election of a Subcommittee vice chair. Consent: Item 1 minutes of the March 4 meeting, Item 2 RHNA Timeline. Information and discussion: Item 3 Topic Outlook, Item 4 Comments Received Relating to the 6th Cycle RHNA, Item 5 HCD update (Megan Kirkeby, Assistant Deputy Director of Fair Housing, 30 minutes), Item 6 RHNA Distribution Methodology and Social Equity (Ma'Ayn Johnson, 40 minutes). Staff report: Panel of Experts March 27, 2019 session update.","detail":["The agenda lists the previous minutes as \"March 4, 2018\"; the minutes themselves are dated March 4, 2019","Next regular meeting: May 6, 2019, 10 AM"],"qs":[],"rel":["pf_5","pf_6"],"outcome":""},{"id":"pf_1","mtg":"m20190401","grp":"Consent calendar","title":"Item 1. Minutes of the March 4, 2019 meeting","pages":[9,13],"sum":"Record of the previous meeting. The consent calendar passed with one abstention (Margaret Finlay, Los Angeles County), and the Subregional Delegation Guidelines were approved to go to CEHD. The Subcommittee approved distributing the Local Planning Factor Survey (14 factors covering planning opportunities and constraints), due April 30, 2019. Staff said they would convene a panel of experts on estimating existing housing need. Item 6 was a discussion of overarching principles for social equity distribution, including the 110% adjustment from the prior cycle; factors discussed were jobs-housing balance, high housing costs and transit access.","detail":["Public comment by Paavo Monkkonen and John Mirisch (Beverly Hills) on where rezoned sites should go and who should get preference","Adjourned at 11:56 AM"],"qs":["The 14-factor local planning survey was due April 30, 2019. The 7th cycle surveys are due Jan 29, 2027. What changed between the two?"],"rel":["pf_6","d_jan"],"outcome":"Delegation guidelines and the survey were approved with no noes recorded; Finlay abstained on the consent calendar and was absent for the delegation vote."},{"id":"pf_2","mtg":"m20190401","grp":"Consent calendar","title":"Item 2. RHNA Timeline (receive and file)","pages":[15,16],"sum":"Same timeline format as June: the 6th cycle covers October 2021 through October 2029, with phases for the regional determination (12/2018 to 08/2019) and methodology development (02/2019 to 09/2019).","detail":[],"qs":[],"rel":["pg_2"],"outcome":""},{"id":"pf_3","mtg":"m20190401","grp":"Consent calendar","title":"Item 3. RHNA Subcommittee Topic Outlook (receive and file)","pages":[17,18],"sum":"The meeting schedule, revised 03/15/19. Meetings are held on the first Monday of the month unless otherwise noted.","detail":[],"qs":[],"rel":["pf_2"],"outcome":""},{"id":"pf_4","mtg":"m20190401","grp":"Information items","title":"Item 4. Comments Received Relating to the 6th Cycle RHNA (as of 03/20/19)","pages":[19,20],"sum":"Table of written comments with date, name, organization, topic and summary. Includes the City of Mission Viejo (Gail Shiomoto-Lohr, Dec 2, 2018: clarification on legislative amendments to trade and transfer, SCAG's role in methodology and liability in delegating subregions, and overcrowding rates) and the City of Beverly Hills.","detail":[],"qs":[],"rel":["pg_4"],"outcome":""},{"id":"pf_5","mtg":"m20190401","grp":"Information items","title":"Item 5. Update from HCD (Megan Kirkeby)","pages":[21,24],"sum":"HCD's Assistant Deputy Director of Fair Housing presents an update on the RHNA process with statewide legislative and budget updates. The attached HCD PowerPoint lists the statutory objectives of RHNA (more housing and a mix of types and tenure; infill and socioeconomic equity; jobs-housing balance and fit; balancing disproportionate income distributions; affirmatively furthering fair housing) and the steps of the process, flagging \"new factors\" for the HCD determination.","detail":["Fiscal impact: RHNA work is funded from the FY 2018-19 General Fund budget"],"qs":["HCD's slides call out \"new factors\" for the determination. Which of them are now driving the 7th cycle (cost burden, overcrowding, vacancy)?"],"rel":["pg_5"],"outcome":""},{"id":"pf_6","mtg":"m20190401","grp":"Information items","title":"Item 6. RHNA Distribution Methodology and Social Equity (staff report)","pages":[25,39],"sum":"Staff seek input on the mechanism used to distribute need in the proposed methodology. The Subcommittee had said factors beyond household income distribution, such as proximity to transit and jobs-housing fit, should be considered. The report analyzes data by county (access to high quality transit areas, jobs-to-worker ratio by wage, opportunity indices), shows how a social equity adjustment works with a City A example, and reviews other councils of governments (ABAG applied a 175% adjustment in the 4th and 5th cycles; SACOG used a 100% component for very low and low income combined, aiming at regional income parity by 2050). Staff would give a recommendation on May 6 and expected the proposed methodology to be ready for public comment by September 2019.","detail":["State housing law sets five objectives (including allocating a lower proportion to an income category where a jurisdiction already has a disproportionately high share, and affirmatively furthering fair housing) but leaves the method to each COG (p.27)","Median household income by county (ACS 2013-2017): Imperial $44,779; Los Angeles $61,015; Orange $81,851; Riverside $60,807; San Bernardino $57,156; Ventura $81,972; SCAG region $64,989 (p.29)","In 2015 the earlier reform Subcommittee's final report said the 110% adjustment should be reviewed for future cycles (p.37)","The regional determination for the 6th cycle was expected no later than August 2019 (p.27)"],"qs":["ABAG used 175% where SCAG used 110%. How would a higher percentage change the lower-income share assigned to Orange County cities?","Orange County's median income is $81,851, about 26% above the region's $64,989. How does the income-category split use county median income, and does it help or hurt high-income cities with little transit?"],"rel":["pe_4","pg_6","pb_4"],"outcome":"Information item; no vote."},{"id":"pf_ppt","mtg":"m20190401","grp":"Attachments to Item 6","title":"PowerPoint: Regional Distribution and Social Equity Factors in RHNA Methodology (9 slides)","pages":[40,48],"sum":"Slide headings: Regional Distribution and Social Equity Factors; Objectives of RHNA; Stages of a RHNA Allocation; Regional Projected and Existing Need; Allocation of Regional Existing Need to Local Jurisdictions; Modifying Existing Housing Need (Access to Transit; Jobs-Housing Fit; Opportunity Indices; Weighted Factor); Social Equity Adjustments; Other COG Methodologies.","detail":["Slide bullets are images in the PDF; only headings and a few chart labels could be read"],"qs":[],"rel":["pf_6"],"outcome":""}]},{"id":"m20150318","iso":"2015-03-18","date":"Mar 18, 2015","label":"Meeting 6, final report, 1:00 PM","stem":"rhna031815fullagn","pdfPages":100,"place":"SCAG Main Office, 818 W. 7th Street, 12th Floor Board Room, Los Angeles","chair":"Hon. Bill Jahn (San Bernardino County, Big Bear Lake)","overview":["The action item is staff's Final Report: it compiles the Subcommittee's recommendations from the earlier meetings, by meeting, for the Subcommittee to affirm and send to CEHD and then the Regional Council.","The topics it covers: the RHNA determination process, how the allocation is built for local jurisdictions, revision requests and appeals, and housing element development and funding incentives.","Two recaps ask what the earlier meetings left open: whether there should be a RHNA credit for inclusionary zoning, and how the 110% social equity adjustment works.","The packet also carries updated results of the change-in-circumstances survey, and 38 pages of 2012 RTP/SCS adoption materials with no explanation of why."],"votes":{"text":"The result of the vote on the Final Report is not in the downloaded packets (the minutes of this meeting would be in the next meeting's packet, which is not in the set).","src":null},"items":[{"id":"pe_n","mtg":"m20150318","grp":"Meeting notice and members","title":"Meeting notice and videoconference sites","pages":[1,2],"sum":"Meeting No. 6, Wednesday March 18, 2015, 1:00 to 4:00 PM, SCAG Main Office, 12th Floor Board Room, with video sites including the Orange County Regional Office (600 S. Main Street, Orange).","detail":[],"qs":[],"rel":["pe_m"],"outcome":""},{"id":"pe_m","mtg":"m20150318","grp":"Meeting notice and members","title":"Subcommittee membership and teleconference notice","pages":[3,4],"sum":"Orange County: Hon. Rose Espinoza (La Habra, OCCOG) as primary and Hon. Michele Martinez (Santa Ana) as alternate. (The page header still reads 2013/2014. The earlier Orange County member, Hon. Ron Garcia of Brea, is called a former Subcommittee member in Item 3.)","detail":[],"qs":[],"rel":["pe_3"],"outcome":""},{"id":"pe_a","mtg":"m20150318","grp":"Agenda","title":"Agenda","pages":[5,6],"sum":"Consent calendar: Item 1 minutes of Sept 29, 2014; Item 2 Topic Outlook; Item 3 Recap of Credit for Inclusionary Zoning; Item 4 Recap of Calculating Low and Very Low Income Housing Need in RHNA Methodology. Action item 5: Final Report, Recommendations Relating to RHNA and Housing Element Reform (Huasha Liu, 30 minutes): review and affirm the recommendations made in the prior four meetings for review by CEHD and approval by the Regional Council.","detail":[],"qs":[],"rel":["pe_5"],"outcome":""},{"id":"pe_1","mtg":"m20150318","grp":"Consent calendar","title":"Item 1. Minutes of the Sept 29, 2014 meeting","pages":[7,12],"sum":"Record of Meeting 5. The Chair took Action Item 4 first because two members had to leave early. The survey on \"significant and unforeseen change in circumstances\" was sent July 31, 2014 with a due date of Aug 22; the minutes record 70 responses, about 50 of which completed at least one question (the Sept staff report counted 54 completed). Staff recommended HCD formalize streamlined review using pre-approved existing-need data sets, and keep inclusionary zoning creditable in housing elements.","detail":["Two motions are recorded, both unanimous"],"qs":[],"rel":["pd_3","pd_4"],"outcome":"Both motions in the minutes were approved unanimously."},{"id":"pe_2","mtg":"m20150318","grp":"Consent calendar","title":"Item 2. RHNA and Housing Element Reform Topic Outlook","pages":[13,14],"sum":"Updated outlook of the six subcommittee meetings; Meeting 6 is shown as March 18, 2015, summary of discussion and approval of recommended actions.","detail":[],"qs":[],"rel":["pe_5"],"outcome":""},{"id":"pe_3","mtg":"m20150318","grp":"Consent calendar","title":"Item 3. Recap of Credit for Inclusionary Zoning","pages":[15,20],"sum":"At the Sept 29 meeting HCD said that instead of an automatic RHNA credit from SCAG, a jurisdiction can include an inclusionary zoning ordinance in its sites and zoning analysis when it prepares its housing element, and that HCD would take up the topic in a statewide housing working group expected in 2015. Staff prepared this report in response to ongoing dialogue by former member Hon. Ron Garcia on possible inclusionary zoning legislation. Staff repeat that a RHNA credit would be uncertain because an ordinance is tied to specific zoning, development and construction, which the RHNA allocation is not.","detail":["Pages 17 to 20 are an American Planning Association PAS QuickNotes attachment on inclusionary housing; its text is garbled in the PDF, open the pages to read it"],"qs":["The 2015 position was that inclusionary zoning counts in the housing element, not the RHNA number. Has anything in state law since changed that?"],"rel":["pd_4","pb_4"],"outcome":"For information only; no action."},{"id":"pe_4","mtg":"m20150318","grp":"Consent calendar","title":"Item 4. Recap of Calculating Low and Very Low Income Housing Need in RHNA Methodology","pages":[21,24],"sum":"Explains the 110% social equity adjustment adopted by the Regional Council for both the 4th and 5th cycles. It compares a city's income mix to its county's and adjusts by 110%. With example City A (very low 29.5% vs county 25.3%), the adjusted very low share falls to about 24.9% and the above moderate share rises from 30.5% to 43.5%. The Subcommittee had recommended on March 13, 2014 reviewing the methodology as part of the 6th cycle starting in 2018, and a member asked for another briefing on Sept 29.","detail":[],"qs":["The 110% adjustment was the only factor affecting the distribution of need in the 5th cycle. Which factors does the 7th cycle methodology add or change?"],"rel":["pb_4","pf_6","pg_6"],"outcome":"For information only; no action."},{"id":"pe_5","mtg":"m20150318","grp":"Action item","title":"Item 5. Final Report: Recommendations Relating to RHNA and Housing Element Reform","pages":[25,52],"sum":"Recommended action: review and affirm the recommendations made in the prior four meetings for CEHD review and Regional Council approval. The report lists the Subcommittee's actions meeting by meeting: Meeting 2 (SCAG process, tribal land, the 3% DOF margin), Meeting 3 (allocation development: communication protocols, local input, trade and transfer, methodology), Meeting 4 (revision requests and appeals) and Meeting 5 (housing element development and funding). Staff propose one amendment: the 3% DOF margin fix, once expected in a 2014 Omnibus Bill, is now expected to be part of HCD's comprehensive statewide reform, so staff would seek it in a future Omnibus Bill. Pages 30 to 52 are the Reform Matrix (version 02/27/15).","detail":["Item 7a: establishing overarching principles for the 6th cycle is unnecessary; the bottom-up local review process used for the most recent RTP/SCS and the practice of accepting local input is the basis (p.27)","Item 11: the 6th cycle charter will keep the option of ex-officio external stakeholders (p.28)","Item 12: SCAG will provide a sample packet for revision requests and appeals (p.28)","Item 21: staff recommend state law on transitional and supportive housing stay unchanged (p.29)","HCD's Glen Campora attended all the Subcommittee meetings (p.26)"],"qs":["Which of the 2015 recommendations were actually carried out in the 6th cycle (sample appeal packet, ex-officio members, trade-and-transfer template)?","Staff said the vacancy-credit accuracy issue would be addressed in the 6th cycle only if the credit is granted again. Was it?"],"rel":["pa_4","pb_4","pc_4","pd_4","pe_survey"],"outcome":"The result of the vote is not in the downloaded packets."},{"id":"pe_survey","mtg":"m20150318","grp":"Attachments to Item 5","title":"Updated Unforeseen and Significant Change in Circumstance Survey Results","pages":[53,62],"sum":"Staff update the survey on what could count as a \"significant and unforeseen change in circumstances\" as a basis for a RHNA appeal. Of the 13 appeals in the 5th cycle, six cited it and none succeeded. There were 53 individual answers to Question 1, including local planning constraints, annexation of county land, dissolution of redevelopment funding and land use changes outside a jurisdiction's control. The top end-date answer was \"at the final RHNA adoption\" (18 users). To be included in the Subcommittee's final recommendations.","detail":["Pages 57 to 62 are the survey form and the written-in answers"],"qs":["The survey found no agreed definition. Has HCD or the Legislature since defined it?"],"rel":["pd_3","pc_4"],"outcome":""},{"id":"pe_rtp","mtg":"m20150318","grp":"Other materials in the packet","title":"2012 RTP/SCS adoption materials (Regional Council report, executive summary, Resolution 12-538-2)","pages":[63,100],"sum":"Not subcommittee business: the April 4, 2012 staff report to the Regional Council on the Proposed Final 2012-2035 RTP/SCS, the plan's executive summary, and Resolution No. 12-538-2 approving the plan. The final report refers to the bottom-up local review process used for the most recent RTP/SCS (item 7a), which may be why these are attached; the packet does not say.","detail":[],"qs":[],"rel":["pe_5"],"outcome":""}]},{"id":"m20140929","iso":"2014-09-29","date":"Sep 29, 2014","label":"Meeting 5, 1:00 PM","stem":"rhna092914fullagn","pdfPages":54,"place":"SCAG Main Office, 818 W. 7th Street, 12th Floor Board Room, Los Angeles","chair":"Hon. Bill Jahn (San Bernardino County, Big Bear Lake)","overview":["Staff reported the results of the survey on what a \"significant and unforeseen change in circumstances\" could be (item 3).","The action item covered nine housing element topics where SCAG would work with HCD: streamlined review and pre-approved data, inclusionary zoning credit, community design, hardship extensions, default density, transitional and supportive housing, funding, incentives and CEQA exemptions (item 4).","Two of the topics came from named Southern California cities: Oxnard and the County of Riverside asked for a hardship process for more time to adopt housing elements, and Oxnard asked for state funding for RHNA and housing element preparation.","The minutes of the May 29, 2014 meeting are included."],"votes":{"text":"Per the minutes in the March 18, 2015 packet (pp.7-12): both motions recorded for this meeting were approved unanimously, and the Chair took the action item first because two members had to leave early.","src":"m20150318","srcPages":[7,12],"srcStem":"rhna031815fullagn","srcDate":"Mar 18, 2015"},"items":[{"id":"pd_n","mtg":"m20140929","grp":"Meeting notice and members","title":"Meeting notice, videoconference sites and members","pages":[1,3],"sum":"Meeting No. 5, Monday September 29, 2014, 1:00 to 4:00 PM, with video sites including the Orange County Regional Office. The 2013/2014 Subcommittee member list is on page 3.","detail":[],"qs":[],"rel":["pd_a"],"outcome":""},{"id":"pd_a","mtg":"m20140929","grp":"Agenda","title":"Agenda","pages":[5,6],"sum":"Consent: Item 1 minutes of May 29, 2014; Item 2 Topic Outlook and Matrix. Action items: Item 3 Unforeseen and Significant Change in Circumstances Survey (Ma'Ayn Johnson, 15 minutes; recommended action: include the survey results in the Subcommittee's final report) and Item 4 Issues and Recommendations Relating to RHNA and Housing Element Reform (Huasha Liu, 50 minutes).","detail":[],"qs":[],"rel":["pd_3","pd_4"],"outcome":""},{"id":"pd_1","mtg":"m20140929","grp":"Consent calendar","title":"Item 1. Minutes of the May 29, 2014 meeting","pages":[7,11],"sum":"Record of Meeting 4. The consent calendar passed unanimously. HCD's Glen Campora said HCD was weighing a legislative fix for the difference between DOF and COG population forecasts but had two weeks left to decide on a 2014 Omnibus Bill, and might wait to propose a more comprehensive set of amendments after a self-assessment by the MPOs. Staff presented the five appeals topics. The Subcommittee approved the staff recommendations unanimously (ayes Finlay, Garcia, Franklin, McCallon, Morehouse, Viegas-Walker).","detail":["Hon. Ron Garcia (Brea, OCCOG) asked that staff reports use the full phrase \"significant and unforeseen\" and not only \"unforeseen\"; Huasha Liu agreed","HCD clarified that it would facilitate the conversation on a definition but that a definition would come by legislative amendment, not an HCD administrative change"],"qs":[],"rel":["pc_4","pd_3"],"outcome":"Approved unanimously (6 ayes, no noes, no abstentions)."},{"id":"pd_2","mtg":"m20140929","grp":"Consent calendar","title":"Item 2. RHNA and Housing Element Reform Topic Outlook and Matrix","pages":[13,28],"sum":"Updated outlook (pages 13 to 14) and the Reform Matrix of topics, version 3/3/14 (pages 15 to 28).","detail":[],"qs":[],"rel":["pa_3"],"outcome":""},{"id":"pd_3","mtg":"m20140929","grp":"Action items","title":"Item 3. Unforeseen and Significant Change in Circumstance Survey","pages":[29,38],"sum":"At its May 29 meeting the Subcommittee asked staff to survey all jurisdictions on what could count as a \"significant and unforeseen change in circumstances\", one of the grounds for a RHNA appeal. In the 5th cycle, 6 of the 13 appeals cited it and none was granted. The survey went to city and county managers and planning directors on July 31, 2014, due Aug 22. As of Sept 17, 54 completed responses had come in. Examples beyond a city's control: natural disasters, court orders, newly identified health or safety hazards, and acquisition of land by the state or federal government. For the earliest start date, 15 chose \"at the draft RHNA allocation\", 13 chose \"between the local planning factor survey and methodology adoption\", and 9 chose Other; for the latest end date, 17 chose \"at the final RHNA adoption\".","detail":["HCD's Glen Campora said in October 2013 that such a change must relate to land use planning","The survey form is on pages 34 to 36 and the answer summaries on pages 37 to 38","The minutes of this meeting (in the March 2015 packet) say 70 responses with about 50 completing at least one question; the staff report counts 54 completed responses"],"qs":["What would a definition have to say to be usable by an Appeals Board that rejected every change-in-circumstances appeal in the 4th and 5th cycles?"],"rel":["pc_4","pe_survey","pe_5"],"outcome":"Recorded in the March 2015 minutes: both motions approved unanimously."},{"id":"pd_4","mtg":"m20140929","grp":"Action items","title":"Item 4. Issues and Recommendations Relating to RHNA and Housing Element Reform","pages":[39,54],"sum":"Staff recommended actions on nine topics, mostly asking HCD for changes: (1) formalize streamlined review with pre-approved existing-needs data sets like the 5th cycle; (2) keep supporting inclusionary zoning counting toward housing element needs; (3) facilitate HCD and city discussion on community design; (4) provide information on timelines and facilitate talks with HCD for cities needing more time; (5) facilitate discussion on default density; (6) leave state law on transitional and supportive housing unchanged; (7) keep working with HCD and others on state-level funding; (8) coordinate on incentives for compliant housing elements; (9) continue to provide CEQA streamlining information (SB 743).","detail":["Topic 4: the hardship request came from Oxnard and the County of Riverside (p.44)","Topic 7: for the 4th and 5th cycles SCAG charged non-member jurisdictions for RHNA development but not member jurisdictions (p.47)","Pages 50 to 54 carry no readable text in the PDF; they appear to be attachment pages"],"qs":["Topic 4 asked for a hardship process to get more time on housing elements. Does any such process exist in current law, and does it matter for Orange County's October 2029 deadline?","Topic 7 noted RHNA and housing element work is a state mandate without state funding. Has state funding for 7th cycle planning changed that?"],"rel":["pe_3","pe_5","pb_4"],"outcome":"Recorded in the March 2015 minutes: approved unanimously."}]},{"id":"m20140529","iso":"2014-05-29","date":"May 29, 2014","label":"Meeting 4, 10:00 AM","stem":"rhna052914fullagn","pdfPages":40,"place":"SCAG Main Office, 818 W. 7th Street, 12th Floor Board Room, Los Angeles","chair":"Hon. Bill Jahn (San Bernardino County, Big Bear Lake)","overview":["The topic was how RHNA revision requests and appeals work, and whether they should change before the 6th cycle.","In the 5th cycle there were 14 revision requests and 13 appeals. Of 7,378 units requested in revision requests, 149 were granted and deducted from the regional total; none of the 4,287 units requested on appeal were granted.","Staff recommended keeping the option of non-voting ex-officio stakeholders on the subcommittee and appeals board, instead of a separate neutral third-party hearing board.","Staff also proposed surveying jurisdictions on what counts as a \"significant and unforeseen change in circumstances\"."],"votes":{"text":"Per the minutes in the September 29, 2014 packet (pp.7-11): the consent calendar and the staff recommendations were each approved unanimously, 6 ayes (Finlay, Garcia, Franklin, McCallon, Morehouse, Viegas-Walker), no noes, no abstentions.","src":"m20140929","srcPages":[7,11],"srcStem":"rhna092914fullagn","srcDate":"Sep 29, 2014"},"items":[{"id":"pc_n","mtg":"m20140529","grp":"Meeting notice and members","title":"Meeting notice and members","pages":[1,3],"sum":"Meeting No. 4, Thursday May 29, 2014, 10:00 AM to 1:00 PM, with video sites including the Orange County Regional Office. 2013/2014 member list on page 3.","detail":[],"qs":[],"rel":["pc_a"],"outcome":""},{"id":"pc_a","mtg":"m20140529","grp":"Agenda","title":"Agenda","pages":[4,5],"sum":"Consent: Item 1 minutes of March 13, 2014; Item 2 Topic Outlook/Matrix. Information: Item 3 HCD status update (verbal, 30 minutes). Action: Item 4 Issues and Recommendations Relating to RHNA and Housing Element Reform (Huasha Liu, 50 minutes).","detail":[],"qs":[],"rel":["pc_4"],"outcome":""},{"id":"pc_1","mtg":"m20140529","grp":"Consent calendar","title":"Item 1. Minutes of the March 13, 2014 meeting","pages":[6,12],"sum":"Record of Meeting 3. Six motions are recorded and all passed unanimously by county roll call. They approved: continuing SCAG's local review and input communication protocols (A7); finding that overarching principles for local input are not necessary and directing staff to record the history of accepting local input in the final report (A1); continuing to facilitate talks with HCD so inclusionary zoning can count in housing elements (B9); and encouraging trade-and-transfer and developing a sample agreement (A2). Staff noted no trade-and-transfer agreements were received in the 4th or 5th cycles.","detail":["For the 5th cycle local input ran from 2009 to 2011; the preliminary draft allocation data went to jurisdictions in May 2011 and the formal draft allocation in February 2012","Staff said the vacancy credit was a one-time regional excess vacancy credit of 75,391 units and that it is premature to say whether HCD will grant one again"],"qs":[],"rel":["pb_4"],"outcome":"All six motions were recorded as unanimous (no noes or abstentions)."},{"id":"pc_2","mtg":"m20140529","grp":"Consent calendar","title":"Item 2. RHNA and Housing Element Reform Topic Outlook and Matrix","pages":[14,29],"sum":"Updated outlook (pages 14 to 15) and Reform Matrix, version 3/3/14 (pages 16 to 29).","detail":[],"qs":[],"rel":["pa_3"],"outcome":""},{"id":"pc_3","mtg":"m20140529","grp":"Information items","title":"Item 3. HCD status update (verbal)","pages":null,"sum":"No pages in this packet. According to the minutes (in the September 29 packet), HCD's Glen Campora said HCD was considering a legislative amendment on the difference between DOF and COG population forecasts but was undecided, partly waiting for the MPOs' self-assessment report, and might propose a more comprehensive set of amendments rather than piecemeal items.","detail":[],"qs":[],"rel":["pa_3","pb_3","pd_1"],"outcome":"Information item."},{"id":"pc_4","mtg":"m20140529","grp":"Action items","title":"Item 4. Issues and Recommendations Relating to RHNA and Housing Element Reform","pages":[30,40],"sum":"Five topics on revision requests and appeals, with staff recommendations: (1) a neutral third-party hearing board (A4): keep the charter option for ex-officio external stakeholders instead; (2) a template or samples of successful requests (A9): provide a sample packet and past granted examples for the 6th cycle; (3) posting staff responses to requests and appeals (A6): keep meeting the legal notice requirements and give as much lead time as possible; (4) the process timeline (C1): keep revision requests and appeals as separate processes as state law provides; (5) the definition of change in circumstances (B6): survey jurisdictions and share the results with HCD and other MPOs. In the 5th cycle, the revision and appeals processes ran from the draft allocation in February 2012 to the Appeals Board's decisions in July 2012.","detail":["5th cycle: fourteen (14) revision requests and thirteen (13) appeals; 149 of 7,378 units granted on revision; none of 4,287 units granted on appeal (p.32)","Attachments (pages 38 to 40) are the 5th cycle revision request and appeal timelines"],"qs":["Appeals Board members are elected officials from the Regional Council. Staff noted they might be seen as having a bias. What guardrails does the 7th cycle appeals process have?","Only 149 of 7,378 units were granted on revision and none on appeal. What does that say about how much an appeal can change a city's number?"],"rel":["pd_3","pe_5","pe_survey"],"outcome":"Approved unanimously per the September 29 minutes."}]},{"id":"m20140313","iso":"2014-03-13","date":"Mar 13, 2014","label":"Meeting 3, 1:00 PM","stem":"rhna031314fullagn","pdfPages":39,"place":"SCAG Main Office, 818 W. 7th Street, 12th Floor Board Room, Los Angeles","chair":"Hon. Bill Jahn (San Bernardino County, Big Bear Lake)","overview":["The topics were about how SCAG builds the RHNA allocation: an earlier preview of the draft allocation, local input on the growth forecast, trading and transferring allocations, General Plan updates, and methodology (the 110% social equity adjustment and the vacancy credit).","Staff recommended against giving cities an automatic RHNA credit for inclusionary zoning ordinances, since a city can count them in its housing element.","No trade-and-transfer agreements were received in the 4th or 5th cycles.","HCD's Glen Campora reported on putting the 3% DOF/COG population margin fix into a 2014 Omnibus Bill."],"votes":{"text":"Per the minutes in the May 29, 2014 packet (pp.6-12): all six motions recorded for this meeting were approved unanimously by county roll call.","src":"m20140529","srcPages":[6,12],"srcStem":"rhna052914fullagn","srcDate":"May 29, 2014"},"items":[{"id":"pb_n","mtg":"m20140313","grp":"Meeting notice and members","title":"Meeting notice, members and teleconference notice","pages":[1,5],"sum":"Meeting No. 3, Thursday March 13, 2014, 1:00 to 4:00 PM, with video sites including the Orange County Regional Office. 2013/2014 member list on page 3.","detail":[],"qs":[],"rel":["pb_a"],"outcome":""},{"id":"pb_a","mtg":"m20140313","grp":"Agenda","title":"Agenda","pages":[7,8],"sum":"Consent: Item 1 minutes of Jan 23, 2014; Item 2 Topic Outlook/Matrix. Information: Item 3 HCD status update (30 minutes). Action: Item 4 Issues and Recommendations Relating to RHNA and Housing Element Reform (50 minutes).","detail":[],"qs":[],"rel":["pb_4"],"outcome":""},{"id":"pb_1","mtg":"m20140313","grp":"Consent calendar","title":"Item 1. Minutes of the Jan 23, 2014 meeting","pages":[9,11],"sum":"Record of Meeting 2. The minutes of the first meeting (Oct 23, 2013) were approved with a correction showing Hon. Kathryn McCullough (Orange County alternate, Lake Forest, OCCOG) was present, a point raised by Hon. Ron Garcia (Brea, OCCOG). Staff reminded members that voting is one vote per county. The Subcommittee approved the staff recommendations as amended: live webcasting for 6th cycle meetings, a point of contact the same as the 2016 RTP/SCS process, and educating jurisdictions on the difference between revision requests and appeals.","detail":[],"qs":[],"rel":["pa_4"],"outcome":"Approved unanimously (ayes Finlay, Garcia, Franklin, McCallon, Morehouse, Viegas-Walker)."},{"id":"pb_2","mtg":"m20140313","grp":"Consent calendar","title":"Item 2. RHNA and Housing Element Reform Topic Outlook/Matrix","pages":[13,28],"sum":"Updated outlook (pages 13 to 14) and the Reform Matrix, version 3/3/14 (pages 15 to 28).","detail":[],"qs":[],"rel":["pa_3"],"outcome":""},{"id":"pb_3","mtg":"m20140313","grp":"Information items","title":"Item 3. HCD status update","pages":[29,30],"sum":"Glen Campora (HCD) updates on matrix topic B4, the margin between COG and DOF population projections. HCD notified State legislative staff that it could be fixed in a forthcoming Omnibus Bill this session; next HCD would brief stakeholders, then discuss amendment language with legislative staff. Opportunities for Omnibus Bill amendments were available until June 2014.","detail":[],"qs":[],"rel":["pa_4","pc_3"],"outcome":"Information only."},{"id":"pb_4","mtg":"m20140313","grp":"Action items","title":"Item 4. Issues and Recommendations Relating to RHNA and Housing Element Reform","pages":[31,39],"sum":"Five topics. (1) Preliminary draft of the RHNA allocation (A7): staff say the existing local input process already gives early review; SCAG published preliminary assessments in December 2011 and the formal draft allocation in February 2012. (2) Local input on the growth forecast (A1, A8, B9): staff do not recommend a RHNA credit for inclusionary zoning. (3) Trade and transfer (A2): develop a sample agreement. (4) General Plan updates (B5): make sure jurisdictions know the data submission timelines. (5) Methodology (A11, A12, C8): the 110% social equity adjustment, explained with City A (very low 29.5% falls to 24.9%; above moderate 30.5% rises to 43.5%), and the one-time vacancy credit.","detail":["The 5th cycle regional excess vacancy credit was 75,391 units (p.39)","Pages 36 to 37 recount that no trade and transfer agreements were received in the 4th or 5th cycles","The Subcommittee recommended reviewing the methodology as part of the 6th cycle starting in 2018 (from the March 2015 recap)"],"qs":["Staff said giving a RHNA credit for inclusionary zoning would be uncertain because it depends on zoning and construction. How does that argument apply to the 7th cycle?","The 5th cycle had a one-time vacancy credit of 75,391 units. How does vacancy enter the 7th cycle determination?"],"rel":["pe_4","pf_6","pe_3","pc_1"],"outcome":"All motions were recorded as unanimous (May 29 packet minutes)."}]},{"id":"m20140123","iso":"2014-01-23","date":"Jan 23, 2014","label":"Meeting 2, 1:00 PM","stem":"rhna012314fullagn","pdfPages":40,"place":"SCAG Main Office, 818 W. 7th Street, 12th Floor Board Room, Los Angeles","chair":"Hon. Bill Jahn (San Bernardino County, Big Bear Lake)","overview":["Second meeting of the RHNA and Housing Element Reform Subcommittee, which SCAG set up after the 5th cycle to review concerns raised in that cycle and give staff guidance before the 6th cycle.","Staff sorted every concern into three routes: SCAG process refinements, HCD administrative changes, and legislative changes (the Topic Outlook and Matrix).","The action item covered five topics: live webcasting, a point of contact for planning directors, funding for jurisdictions that accept RHNA delegation, growth on tribal lands, and the 3% allowable difference between SCAG and DOF population projections.","Orange County's seats were held by Hon. Ron Garcia (Brea, OCCOG) and Hon. Kathryn McCullough (Lake Forest, OCCOG)."],"votes":{"text":"Per the minutes in the March 13, 2014 packet (pp.9-11): the consent calendar and the action item were each approved unanimously, with ayes from Finlay, Garcia, Franklin, McCallon, Morehouse and Viegas-Walker.","src":"m20140313","srcPages":[9,11],"srcStem":"rhna031314fullagn","srcDate":"Mar 13, 2014"},"items":[{"id":"pa_n","mtg":"m20140123","grp":"Meeting notice and members","title":"Meeting notice and videoconference sites","pages":[1,2],"sum":"Meeting No. 2, Thursday January 23, 2014, 1:00 to 4:00 PM, SCAG Main Office (818 W. 7th Street, 12th Floor Board Room, Los Angeles). Video and teleconference sites include HCD in Sacramento and the Orange County Regional Office (600 S. Main Street, Suite 912, Orange). Contact: Ma'Ayn Johnson, (213) 236-1975.","detail":[],"qs":[],"rel":["pa_m"],"outcome":""},{"id":"pa_m","mtg":"m20140123","grp":"Meeting notice and members","title":"Subcommittee membership (2013/2014)","pages":[5,5],"sum":"Orange County: Hon. Ron Garcia (Brea, OCCOG) as primary and Hon. Kathryn McCullough (Lake Forest, OCCOG) as alternate. The Chair is Hon. Bill Jahn of Big Bear Lake (San Bernardino County).","detail":[],"qs":[],"rel":["pa_n"],"outcome":""},{"id":"pa_a","mtg":"m20140123","grp":"Agenda","title":"Agenda","pages":[7,8],"sum":"Consent: Item 1 minutes of the Oct 23, 2013 meeting. Information: Item 2 Updated Topic Outlook (45 minutes) and Item 3 HCD status update (45 minutes). Action: Item 4 Issues and Recommendations Relating to RHNA and Housing Element Reform (50 minutes).","detail":[],"qs":[],"rel":["pa_4"],"outcome":""},{"id":"pa_1","mtg":"m20140123","grp":"Consent calendar","title":"Item 1. Minutes of the Oct 23, 2013 meeting (Meeting 1)","pages":[9,11],"sum":"Record of the first meeting. Staff gave an overview of the 5th cycle: housing elements were due by Oct 15, 2013, and a jurisdiction not adopting by Feb 2014 would have to go to a four-year instead of an eight-year housing element. SCAG Chief Counsel Joann Africa presented the Subcommittee charter; the Subcommittee unanimously recommended it with an added sentence that recommendations be prioritized and separated into administrative and legislative changes. Staff presented the matrix of concerns in three categories and HCD's Glen Campora gave initial feedback; the Subcommittee approved the topics and invited additional topics by Nov 15, 2013.","detail":["Public comment: a Pasadena resident asked that the Subcommittee consider HCD's interpretation of transitional and supportive housing"],"qs":[],"rel":["pa_3"],"outcome":"Charter, topic outlook and the receive-and-file item were each approved unanimously."},{"id":"pa_2","mtg":"m20140123","grp":"Information items","title":"Item 2. Updated Topic Outlook","pages":[13,16],"sum":"Staff added stakeholder comments received by Nov 15, 2013; seven stakeholders submitted additional clarification and topics, such as methodology (A11, A12, C8), inclusionary zoning (B9), mixed-use zoning in RHNA allocations (B10), transitional and supportive housing (B11), CEQA exemptions for affordable infill (C7), suburban definitions for default density (C5) and funding for affordable housing covenants (D2). Expenses for staff and legal support came from General Fund reserves until the FY 13-14 budget was amended.","detail":["Attachment 1 (pages 15 to 16) lays out the topics by meeting, Meeting 1 on Oct 23, 2013 through Meeting 6 proposed for 2014"],"qs":[],"rel":["pa_3"],"outcome":"Information only; no action."},{"id":"pa_3","mtg":"m20140123","grp":"Information items","title":"Item 2, Attachment 2. RHNA and Housing Element Reform Matrix (14 pages)","pages":[17,30],"sum":"The matrix sorts every concern raised in the 5th cycle into (A) SCAG process refinement, (B) HCD administrative changes and (C) legislative changes, with a section D for programs outside state housing law. Each row shows the suggested reform and who suggested it (for example A1 lists SCAG staff, Ojai, Sierra Madre, Calabasas, Oxnard and the County of Ventura), existing policy, and staff's initial response. Dated 12/26/13.","detail":[],"qs":["Which of the matrix topics (default density, streamlined review, trade and transfer, appeals) are still open in the 7th cycle?"],"rel":["pa_2","pe_5","pd_2"],"outcome":""},{"id":"pa_4","mtg":"m20140123","grp":"Information items","title":"Item 3. HCD status update","pages":[31,35],"sum":"Information only. HCD's Glen Campora updates on topics from the first meeting: the margin between HCD and DOF projections (B4), the exemption on tribal lands (B3), housing element streamlined review (B2) and default density ranges (B1, C4, C5, C6). The attachment is an HCD memo to SCAG staff dated Jan 15, 2014 (PDF pages 33 to 35), which has no readable text in the PDF.","detail":[],"qs":[],"rel":["pb_3","pc_3"],"outcome":"Information only."},{"id":"pa_5","mtg":"m20140123","grp":"Action items","title":"Item 4. Issues and Recommendations Relating to RHNA and Housing Element Reform","pages":[36,40],"sum":"Staff recommended five actions for CEHD: (1) explore live webcasting of 6th cycle Subcommittee meetings; (2) keep notifying planning directors, city managers and other stakeholders, with a designated point of contact like the one in the 2016 RTP/SCS local input protocol; (3) keep funding available for jurisdictions that accept RHNA delegation; (4) keep seeking HCD's official confirmation that growth on tribal lands be excluded from regional RHNA allocations; (5) ask for a technical fix in the 2014 Omnibus Bill so the 3% allowable difference between COG and DOF population projections applies to total population.","detail":["Teleconferencing: because of the Brown Act, staff recommended remote participation stay limited to Subcommittee members, with a live webcast as the way to widen access (p.38)","Tribal lands: an HCD letter of Aug 17, 2011 excluded tribal growth for the 5th cycle (p.39)","3% margin: confusion in the 5th cycle over whether the 3% applies to total population or to growth; HCD agreed to apply it to total population (p.40)"],"qs":["Staff sought a point of contact so that city staff turnover did not cause missed notices. Which Orange County contacts has SCAG been given for the 7th cycle?","The 3% DOF rule is the one that gets applied in the 7th cycle's population projections. How did HCD use DOF versus SCAG's forecast in the 2026 determination?"],"rel":["pb_1","pb_3","pe_5"],"outcome":"Approved as amended, unanimously (March 13, 2014 minutes)."}]}],"threads":[{"id":"th_equity","title":"The social equity adjustment (110%)","blurb":"Compares a city's income mix to its county's and moves it 110% of the way past the county mix. Used in the 4th and 5th cycles. In 2019 staff recommended keeping 110% for existing need and using 150% for projected need.","refs":["pb_4","pe_4","pf_6","pg_6","pg_1"]},{"id":"th_appeals","title":"Appeals and \"significant and unforeseen change in circumstances\"","blurb":"The 5th cycle appeals process and the survey to find a workable definition. 6 of 13 appeals cited it and none was granted.","refs":["pc_4","pd_3","pe_survey","pe_5"]},{"id":"th_iz","title":"Credit for inclusionary zoning","blurb":"Staff's position from 2014 to 2015: no automatic RHNA credit; cities count inclusionary ordinances in their housing element's sites analysis.","refs":["pb_4","pc_1","pd_4","pe_3"]},{"id":"th_need","title":"Existing need versus projected need","blurb":"New in the 6th cycle: legislation added measures of existing need (the backlog) alongside growth-driven need. Phasing it across cycles was discussed.","refs":["pf_6","pg_5","pg_6","pg_1"]},{"id":"th_dof","title":"The 3% margin and the Omnibus Bill","blurb":"Whether the 3% allowable difference between SCAG and DOF population forecasts applies to total population. HCD agreed for the 5th cycle; the technical fix was first aimed at the 2014 Omnibus Bill, then folded into broader reform.","refs":["pa_5","pa_4","pb_3","pc_3","pd_1","pe_5"]}]};
